| **08-31** | 🔴🔴 **UBS · Timothy Arcuri — "Download From Hot Chips 2026": the [[OPENAI]] "Jalapeño" ASIC is AVGO's, and UBS uses it to call the MediaTek-over-AVGO trade wrong** | produto / competicao | **bull** | ★ **The single most AVGO-favourable line in the note, stated as a rebuke to the market:** OpenAI presented its **Jalapeño** inference ASIC, and *"to us, the chip AGAIN HIGHLIGHTS THAT INVESTORS ARE OFTEN TOO QUICK TO SIDE WITH MEDIATEK (AND OTHER ASIC SUPPLIERS) OVER AVGO FOR ADVANCED ASIC DESIGN."* 🔴 **THE SPECS AND THE TIMELINE, which are the evidence:** Jalapeño runs at **~700W versus ~1.2kW for GB200**, uses **multi-token prediction** and a **memory-sliced architecture optimised around KV-cache locality**; system level is **128-chip racks, 16 racks into 2,048-chip pods using Ethernet scale-up**. **OAI and AVGO developed it in a very condensed timeline — 9 MONTHS FOR INITIAL RTL — with compute chips built from scratch, while OAI indicated it relied on AVGO for SOME INTERFACE IP AND THE I/O DIE.** ⚠️ **Read the division of labour before treating this as full-stack AVGO content:** OAI did the compute chips from scratch; AVGO supplied interface IP + I/O die. That is a narrower content share than a classic AVGO XPU engagement, even though the win itself is AVGO's. UBS's framing of what it proves: *"Jalapeño is another proof point that well-designed ASICs can offer compelling cost-of-capital and cost-of-operation advantages for scaled deployments, particularly for FRONTIER MODEL VENDORS WITH SUFFICIENT VOLUME."* ⚠️ **But UBS keeps the ceiling unchanged and it is the number to hold this page against:** *"consistent with our LONG-HELD VIEW THAT ASICS ULTIMATELY SETTLE AT ROUGHLY 30% OF THE ACCELERATOR MARKET,"* with GPUs keeping the majority on programmability, flexibility and ecosystem — OAI itself acknowledged *"the need to explicitly map workloads and models onto the hardware,"* implying *"meaningful onboarding and software complexity relative to GPUs."* 🔴 **OTHER AVGO SOCKETS CONFIRMED IN THE SAME NOTE'S EXHIBIT:** [[GOOG]] **TPU v8t** (Broadcom, pre-training, N3, 9,600 chips/Superpod, 121 EF FP4, 2PB shared HBM) and **TPU v7** (Broadcom); [[META]] **MTIA 300 and MTIA 400** (Broadcom). UBS on MTIA 400: *"consistent with our own view that MTIA 400 ramp will see GREATER VOLUMES AND LARGER REVENUES FOR AVGO"* — while noting it stays META-specific recommendation work and *"we do not believe it will reduce META's reliance on GPUs."* ⚠️ **TPU v8i inference (2-die, 384MB SRAM, 288GB HBM3E) is listed with MediaTek as IC partner** — the split GOOG socket sits in the same exhibit. **NETWORKING:** AVGO's **Thor Ultra 800G NIC** — up to 8-plane multipathing, enhanced RoCE, programmable congestion control, *"demonstrating ~96%+ line-rate collective bandwidth"* — set directly against NVDA's Spectrum-X/BlueField-4 as *"continued competition between Nvidia's vertically integrated fabric and more open Ethernet solutions."* See [themes/custom-asic-tpu](themes/custom-asic-tpu.md). [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/ued50865.html) |
| **08-31** | **Deutsche Bank · Gianmarco Conti — hardware-sector INITIATION (read-through only; no AVGO rating or PT)** | produto / competicao | **neutral** | ⚠️ **Read-through row from a [[LITE]]/[[COHR]]/networking initiation; AVGO is not covered in it.** The AVGO-relevant technical marks DB uses to frame the copper-vs-optics debate: **Broadcom Tomahawk 6** as the reference switch ASIC; the SerDes roadmap put at **224G today → 448G next**, with DB stating *"there is no visible SerDes roadmap beyond 448G"* and that *"every doubling of data rate roughly halves [copper's] usable reach"* — copper surviving intra-rack only through 2028. Power marks cited: **Meta/Broadcom 5.4W per 800G port** vs **NVDA ~4-5W**, against ~15-17W for older generations. DB also notes **switch ASIC is c.45% of the switch BOM** (the basis of its [[CSCO]] Silicon One argument). See [themes/optical-cpo](themes/optical-cpo.md). [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/883478bc_729b_4d0d_a75e_f4962a9e3e10_604.html) |
| **08-31** | 🔴🔴 **UBS · Timothy Arcuri — BUY, PT CUT to $470 (from $485)** — "Earnings Previews: GTLB, DELL, MDB, AVGO, HPE…" relayed by UBS sector sales (Robert Ruple); sentiment 7.5/10, implied vol 6.3% | demanda / guidance / competicao | **mixed** | ★★ 🔴🔴 **THE FIRST PT CUT ON THIS PAGE THIS QUARTER, AND THE REASON IS A UNIT NUMBER, NOT A MULTIPLE: *"we are trimming ~$5B from our F2027 AI revenue number (now ~$130B) as **our checks indicate we have been TOO AGGRESSIVE on AVGO's TPU UNIT SHIPMENTS and we now see 6.4MM shipments in CY27**."*** ➜ **6.4mn CY27 TPU units is the first hard unit mark from a US house on this page, and it is a CUT. Read it directly against the Asia desks on [[MEDIATEK]] — Jefferies/Chang's 12-15M TPUs at Google in 2028 and BofA's 8-9mn 2027 programme volume are PROGRAMME totals; UBS's 6.4mn is AVGO's OWN CY27 shipments. Not contradictory, but it means UBS is implicitly conceding meaningful non-AVGO volume inside a programme it still calls "plan of record".** 🔴 **THE QUARTER: AI revenue **$16bn in FQ3 (Jul)**, ramping to **$21-22bn in FQ4**, with FQ4 (Oct) total revenue guided to **~$34bn**; "modest upside" to FQ3 revenue; custom compute continuing to outgrow AI networking. ⚠️ **Reconciles almost exactly with MS/Moore's split already on this page (July AI $16.0bn → October $21.2bn) — two houses independently on the same quarterly shape, which raises the information content of a miss.** 🔴🔴 **NET-NEW AND THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE: [[ANTHROPIC]] IS NOW A QUANTIFIED AVGO CUSTOMER — *"we believe FQ3 was the **FIRST QUARTER OF MEANINGFUL ANTHROPIC TPU SHIPMENTS**, which we estimate contributed **~$2.5B in the quarter**, ramping to **$3.3B in FQ4** and reaching **~$9.3B of total shipments in C2026**."*** ➜ **~$9.3bn of C2026 AVGO revenue attributable to a single private lab. Cross-file to [[ANTHROPIC]] and [[GOOG]] — and note it is the supply side of the FT's same-day *"more than $150bn of AI chips [supplied by Google] to Anthropic"*, i.e. this page finally has a dollar bridge into that financing programme.** **Google: GCP-related AI revenues +~$2bn q/q in FQ3, then +~$1.5bn q/q in FQ4.** **[[OPENAI]]: ~$1.25bn C2026 → ~$12bn C2027. [[META]]: custom-compute shipments "largely flat through F2026 before a more meaningful ramp in F2027". [[AAPL]]: "ramp occurring at a slower pace."** ✅ **UBS clears the [[MRVL]] noise the same way JPM Asia did: *"we do NOT see this development cannibalizing AVGO's share or opportunity within the Google TPU program"*, and expects management to *"forcefully address that it remains **PLAN OF RECORD**"* — a specific, checkable phrase to score Wednesday's call against.** **Guide expectations: NO update to the F2027 >$100bn AI framework; management to steer investors to **F2028 at ~$200bn**.** ⚠️⚠️ **THE CONSTRAINT DISAGREEMENT, AND IT IS NEW AND MATERIAL: UBS says *"we see the timing on its AI revenue ramp being tied to readiness of **POWERED-SHELL INFRASTRUCTURE** rather than compute silicon availability."* **That is the OPPOSITE of Jefferies' 08-31 mechanism already on this page — *"we just don't think there is much more additional SUPPLY to be meaningfully above that"*, i.e. FY27 is wafer/packaging/HBM-capped. Both cannot be the binding constraint. NOT netted: it changes what you watch (datacentre shell commissioning vs CoWoS/HBM allocation) and it changes who else is long the same bottleneck.** **UBS's six earnings topics: (1) custom-ASIC trajectory and XPU program ramps; (2) Anthropic TPU shipments; (3) OpenAI Jalapeño ramp in FY26+; (4) AVGO's position in the TPU program given MediaTek and the new MRVL non-TPU accelerator program; (5) AI networking and scale-up incl. CPO; (6) **update on XPV financing progress**. Arcuri is "doubtful this report will sway the overarching debate on the stock around Google and custom silicon in general."** |
| **08-31** | ⚠️ **Barclays · Tom O'Malley — "what out of hot chips matters most for stocks this Fall"** (semis desk note) | produto / competicao | **mixed** | 🔴 **THE JALAPEÑO TAPE-OUT CONFLICT THIS PAGE FLAGGED ON 08-31 IS RESOLVED, AND IT RESOLVES IN FAVOUR OF BOTH SOURCES: Barclays reports from Hot Chips that *"AI's Jalapeño chip was brought to **TAPE-OUT FROM INITIAL RTL in 9 months**"* — RTL-to-tape-out, not design-start-to-tape-out. **That is a DIFFERENT MEASUREMENT WINDOW from this page's dated ~16-month design-to-tape-out sequence, so the SemiAnalysis "nine months" and the page's 16-month chain are compatible and both stand. Prior flag ("flagged, not reconciled; the dated sequence wins") is superseded — see `## Changelog`.**

⚠️⚠️ **BUT BARCLAYS OPENS THE HARDER QUESTION AND NAMES IT THE KEY ONE: *"engineers discussed developing **most of the IP FROM SCRATCH**. **It is unclear how much AVGO helped with the core design, but we believe this is the key debate from here.**"* ➜ **If OpenAI developed most of the IP itself, AVGO's content per Jalapeño generation is closer to physical implementation + the scale-up fabric than to architecture — which is exactly the CoT-disintermediation risk this page tracks at Google, arriving at a second customer. UBS's C2027 $12bn OpenAI estimate is underwritten by an unresolved content question.**
**ARCHITECTURE, and it is a genuine differentiation point: *"OAI took the OPPOSITE path with Jalapeño"* from the disaggregated-inference direction [[NVDA]] and [[CEREBRAS]] are taking — *"we think this is a TIME TO MARKET decision, and future generations will disaggregate as well. Debate is KV state must move between different hardware in a disaggregated solution and it would rather keep KV local to the same system and flex the active compute, memory, and network based on the requirements of the inference step."*** |
| **08-31** | ⚠️ **UBS (relayed @MF_Camillus, corpus)** — on OpenAI Jalapeño / ASIC adoption | produto / competicao | **bear (for ASIC), bull (for NVDA)** | **THE STRUCTURAL BEAR CASE ON EVERY CUSTOM ASIC, STATED CLEANLY AND WORTH KEEPING ON THIS PAGE PERMANENTLY: *"…many of the same limitations that have historically constrained ASIC adoption remain in place. **OpenAI acknowledged the need to EXPLICITLY MAP WORKLOADS AND MODELS ONTO THE HARDWARE**, while the architecture appears **highly optimized around specific inference characteristics and assumptions regarding KV-cache growth and data movement**. This should create **meaningful onboarding and software complexity relative to GPUs**, which continue to benefit from greater programmability, flexibility, and a much larger ecosystem…"*** ➜ **Pairs exactly with Barclays above: OpenAI chose NOT to disaggregate *because* the chip is tuned to a specific KV-cache/data-movement regime. The flexibility cost and the time-to-market gain are the same design decision seen from two sides. ⚠️ Note UBS is Buy-rated on AVGO — this is not a rating-driven bear note, it is the same house's honest statement of what customers give up.** |
