| **08-31** | 🔴 **Morgan Stanley · Joseph Moore** (w/ Ella Tulchinsky, Mason Wayne, Nicole Kozhukhov, Shane Brett) — "Semiconductors Weekly: Earnings Week 7 (AVGO, AMBA), SWKS Tech Day" · **OW**, Industry View Attractive | demanda / competicao / guidance | **bull** | ★ **THE MOST SPECIFIC QUARTERLY AI SPLIT ANY HOUSE HAS PUT ON THIS PAGE, AND IT IS WHAT TO SCORE THE PRINT AGAINST LINE BY LINE: MS models **July-quarter AI revenue $16.0bn, +48% q/q — $10.8bn custom ASIC + $5.2bn AI networking** — and **October-quarter AI revenue $21.2bn, +32% q/q — $14.9bn custom ASIC + $6.2bn AI networking**, with *"decent room for upside to these numbers as TPU demand remains strong."*** ➜ **A ~$5.2bn/quarter AI-networking run-rate is the first quantification on this page of the *"AI networking ~40% of AI"* ceiling management has guided to — at these marks networking is ~33% of July AI and ~29% of October AI, i.e. BELOW the stated ceiling and falling as ASIC scales.**
**ON THE 2027 FRAMEWORK, MS IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHASING THE BAR: *"we continue to see potential for upside to our roughly **$120bn** AI revenue estimate… We are **not sure management will provide a more formal update** to the 2027 framework this quarter, and **do not think one is necessary** for the fundamental story to remain intact."* The risk statement is explicit: *"Some expectations assume Broadcom will generate **more than $150bn** of AI revenue next year. We think that is possible, but would **not view it as a certainty** versus our roughly $120bn estimate… **The main risk into the print is expectations rather than fundamentals. Last quarter was a good reminder that very strong results are not necessarily enough when the bar is high.**"***
🔴 **THE TPU-SHARE DEFENCE IS REITERATED AND NOW HAS A SPLIT-BY-ROLE, WHICH IS NEW: MS keeps *"Broadcom to retain roughly **80%** of its longer term TPU opportunity"* and calls [[MEDIATEK]] participation *"credible"* — and the MS TMT desk renders Joe's segmentation as **AVGO doing the main TPU · MTK *"likely a merchant chip"* · [[MRVL]] *"low latency [[CEREBRAS]]-like functionality."*** ⚠️⚠️ **THIS IS NOW IN DIRECT, QUANTIFIED CONFLICT WITH JPM ASIA (Gokul Hariharan, same weekend) — see the row below. MS: AVGO keeps ~80% of the TPU opportunity. JPM Asia: MediaTek *"is likely to have MAJORITY UNIT SHARE within TPUv8 (in 2027)"* and be *"the primary ASIC vendor for TPUv9."* Both are kept and NEITHER is netted. They are PARTLY reconcilable — MS's 80% is a REVENUE/opportunity share on an ASP where JPM Asia itself puts AVGO's all-in chip at ~4x MediaTek's, while JPM's claim is a UNIT share — but they are not fully reconcilable, and this is the single biggest open disagreement on this page.**
**Also: MS acknowledges *"recent reports around [[AMD]] involvement in TPU v10"* as evidence GOOGL *"is broadening its supplier ecosystem"*, where JPM Asia's checks say the AMD engagement *"is likely to be mainly for CPUs, rather than accelerators."* ⚠️ **A second live cross-house disagreement, same weekend, same customer.** **On supply noise: *"Reports of downward revisions to certain CoWoS forecasts have created some concern, but these checks can be noisy."*** **AVGO stays MS's *"close number two behind [[NVDA]]"* in AI compute.** ⚠️ **Read-through logged on another page, not this one: the same note carries unconfirmed reports of [[NXPI]] discussions to acquire AMBA.** (Morgan Stanley · Joseph Moore, 2026-08-31) |
| **08-31** | 🔴🔴 **JPM Asia · Gokul Hariharan** (w/ Jennifer Hsieh, David Chou, Jason Chen, Subham Singhania) — [[MEDIATEK]] note *"TPUv10 concerns overdone, ASICs should grow strongly beyond 2028; Stay OW"*, **OW, PT NT$5,600 (▲ from NT$5,300)** ⚠️ **a MediaTek note with NO AVGO rating or PT — AVGO is Harlan Sur's coverage — but it carries the first outside estimate of AVGO's TPU UNIT ECONOMICS this page has ever held** | competicao / margem | **bear** | 🔴🔴 **THE NUMBER: JPM ASIA PUTS AVGO'S ALL-IN TPUv8i ASP AT **~$18,000 PER CHIP ON ~60% ALL-IN GROSS MARGIN** — against MediaTek at **$4,500 on 42% GM** — AND USES IT TO SIZE WHAT [[GOOG]] SAVES BY MOVING A GENERATION TO SEMI-COT: *"Savings per Chip for Google **$7,776**"*, *"**43%** savings with semi-CoT model with Mediatek"*, i.e. *"**~$78bn in chip spend, assuming 10M unit TPU shipment per generation.**"*** ➜ **This is the bear case on this page reduced to arithmetic, and it is uncomfortable precisely because it is NOT a share argument: even if AVGO holds MS's ~80% of the *opportunity*, a $7.8k-per-chip cost wedge is a standing incentive for the customer to keep migrating generations. It is also the clearest explanation yet of WHY Google went to MediaTek at all.** ⚠️⚠️ **GUARDS, AND THEY MATTER: (a) these are **J.P. Morgan ESTIMATES, not disclosed figures** — the table is labelled *"Source: J.P. Morgan estimates"* and AVGO has never disclosed TPU ASP or program margin; (b) the $18k is an **all-in ASP that includes the HBM AVGO does not consign out** (this page carries *"does not consign HBM — integral to the XPU"*), so it is NOT directly comparable to MediaTek's $4.5k — which is exactly why JPM re-bases MediaTek's gross profit for complexity to $3,024 before differencing; (c) the $78bn is per-GENERATION at an assumed 10M units, not per-year.**
🔴 **THE OFFSETTING HALF OF THE SAME NOTE, AND IT IS THE STRONGEST STRUCTURAL ARGUMENT FOR AN AVGO FLOOR THAT HAS APPEARED ALL QUARTER: the SECOND leg — semi-CoT to FULL CoT — is worth only *"~17% or up to **$2.6k per chip** or $26bn"* more, *"requires timely availability of third-party IP such as high-speed Serdes,"* and JPM does *"**not think such a move is realistic before 2030**, given 448G Serdes availability from third party vendors may lag by up to two years."*** ➜ **The cost-out that motivated the move away from AVGO has already been ~75% harvested ($7.8k of a $10.4k total). The remaining prize is small and gated on IP that AVGO and MediaTek own. Read against the 5-year v8-v11 volume anchor from JPM US on the 08-31 research row: the displacement risk is now bounded at BOTH ends.**
🔴 **AND THE ROADMAP DATAPOINT THAT DIRECTLY UPDATES THIS PAGE'S OWN "PUMAFISH CANCELLED?" DEBATE — JPM ASIA NOW SAYS THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF IT AT ALL: *"supply chain indications suggesting **no evidence of a competing Pumafish project from Broadcom in 2028** - supply chain checks suggest only a **Sunfish Ultra project with 4 N3 compute dies and staying at 224G Serdes interconnect**."*** ⚠️ **"Sunfish Ultra, 4× N3 compute dies, 224G SerDes" is net-new to this page and it reads as a *downgrade in ambition* relative to a v9-class part: it stays on N3 while MediaTek's Humufish moves to **N2 compute dies with 336G SerDes**, and it stays at 224G while v10 moves to 448G. If accurate, AVGO's 2028 Google part is a derivative of the v8 line rather than a v9 competitor.** ➜ **This is the FOURTH position on Pumafish this page carries (JPM Hariharan 07-01 *"cancelled/postponed"* · JPM Sur 06-17 / 07-08 *"no delay, v9 on track CY28"* · MS Moore *"~80% retained"* · JPM Hariharan 08-31 *"no evidence, only Sunfish Ultra"*). Two of the four are the SAME HOUSE on opposite sides of the Pacific. Nothing is netted; the contradiction is the finding.** **Also from the same note and rarely quantified: **AVGO opex per employee ~$450k**, vs MediaTek ~$250k and GUC/Alchip ~$150k — the structural cost gap that makes the CoT arithmetic above work.** (J.P. Morgan Asia · Gokul Hariharan, 2026-08-31) |
| **08-28** | 🔴 **Jefferies · Blayne Curtis** (w/ Kevin Garrigan, Crawford Clarke, Andrew Buller) — *"Blayne's Bytes: AVGO/CRDO Previews, Chicago Conference Recap, NVDA/MRVL Recaps"* · **BUY** | guidance / demanda / supply | **mixed** | ★ **THE BEARISH PREVIEW FROM A BULL, AND THE HEADLINE IS THE HONEST ONE: *"Tough to See the Positive Catalyst Until C28 Comes Into View."*** **Numbers: July revenue **$29.6bn (+33% q/q vs Street $29.2bn)**, October guided to **$35.5bn (+20% q/q vs Street $34.7bn)** — a beat-and-raise, modelled.** 🔴 **THE ARGUMENT, AND IT IS A *SUPPLY* ARGUMENT RATHER THAN A DEMAND ONE: *"the setup into the print leans cleaner than last quarter as sentiment/ownership is low but it's still not a positive setup as there is **limited upside to C27 AI estimates**. Expectations are already in the **$130-140B range** for AI Revenue in C27 and **we just don't think there is much more additional supply to be meaningfully above that**."*** ➜ **This is the most important sentence in any of the three previews and it appears in none of the others: Jefferies says the FY27 number is capped by wafers/packaging/HBM, not by orders. If that is right, a bigger guide is not AVAILABLE to management even where the backlog supports it — which is the mechanism that reconciles Harlan's *">$150bn F27 backlog"* with a guide that lands at $120-130bn, and it is the single best explanation on this page for why a beat-and-raise can still sell off.** **On competition: *"we expect AVGO to be the dominant supplier into the TPU ecosystem but **the chatter related to MTK, AMD, and now MRVL are hard to disprove**"* — a materially weaker defence than MS's ~80% or JPM's contractual anchor, and logged as the softest of the three. Upside is dated to C28 *"alongside the deployment of the XPV"*, with *"many moving pieces (memory pricing, supply constraints, etc.)"* making it *"too early for management to frame that opportunity."* Also notes this is **the first earnings call with new CFO Amie Thuener**.** (Jefferies · Blayne Curtis, 2026-08-28) |
| **08-31** | 🔴🔴 **JPM · Harlan Sur** (w/ Apoorva Kumar, Mayur Ramdhani) — "F3Q26 Preview: FY27 AI Revs to Top >$100B Framework; FY28 AI Growth Outlook Accelerating; TPU Competitive Concerns Overblown; AI Customer Diversification Unfolds in CY27; Reit OW" · **OW**, px **$368.79** (28-Aug) ⚠️⚠️ **PRICE TARGET IS INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT IN THE NOTE AS DELIVERED: the alert's structured field reads *"Price Target (Dec-26): $580.00"* while the body text says *"Reiterate OW and **$500 PT**."* BOTH ARE RECORDED. ✅ **RESOLVED 2026-08-31 (same day, see the JPM Buyside Bars row above): the JPM sector specialist quoting Harlan's own basis — *"a $580 TP on 23x $25.35 earnings power exiting F27"* (23 × $25.35 = $583) — closes the arithmetic on **$580**, which is the JPM target this page now carries. The **$500** in the alert body is treated as an error in the alert body and is retained in `## Changelog`. ⚠️ Still a sales relay of a research PT, not the research PDF.** | **competicao / demanda / guidance** | **bull** | ★ 🔴🔴 **THE MOST DIRECT REBUTTAL YET OF THE [[GOOG]]-SHARE-LOSS BEAR, AND IT REFRAMES THE [[MRVL]] DEAL AS ADDITIVE RATHER THAN DISPLACING: *"our channel checks over the last 90 days lead us to believe that **investor concerns around AVGO's competitive position at GOOGL are OVERSTATED**. Instead, we think that the recent partnerships with GOOGL/MRVL and noise around potential suppliers such as **Alchip, GUC**, and others are more reflective of **GOOGL bringing on more partners to SUPPORT ITS INTERNAL COT TEAM** (remember, the COT team has struggled with the rollout of the **v8t/Zebra fish** program) **and to support TPU-ATTACH opportunities**."*** ➜ **This is the third independent house to land on "attach, not displacement" — MS's US desk (Moore, 08-19), MS Asia (Chan, 08-24) and now JPM — and JPM adds the mechanism the others lacked: Google's own customer-owned-tooling team UNDERPERFORMED on v8t/Zebra fish, so the extra partners are backfill for an internal failure. ⚠️ **That directly complicates the COT bear logged on [[MRVL]] on 08-26 (MS Asia: hyperscalers "increasing their own value-add"). JPM's evidence says the COT team stumbled. Both retained, not netted.**
🔴 **THE CONTRACTUAL ANCHOR, WHICH IS THE HARDEST FACT IN THE NOTE: *"we do not see any company displacing AVGO's position as GOOGL's core TPU partner, especially given the terms of the **5-year GOOGL-AVGO agreement signed in early April**, in which **GOOGL committed to ANNUALLY INCREASING TPU-related purchases at AVGO** and **anchored AVGO as the VOLUME PARTNER FOR THE NEXT 4 GENERATIONS OF TPU SKUs (v8-v11)**."*** ➜ **If the v8-v11 volume anchor is right, the Marvell warrant cannot be a core-TPU displacement inside that window by construction — which is exactly what the Barclays 08-27 MRVL note implied when it guessed the already-embedded Google revenue *"is CXL."***
🔴 **THE NUMBERS, AND THE FY27 CALL IS FAR ABOVE MANAGEMENT'S OWN FRAMEWORK: JPM models **$56B+ of AI revenue in FY26** and **2-2.5x y/y growth in FY27 — *"in the $130B+ range even when taking into account tight industry supply dynamics"*** — against management's reiterated **>$100B** framework. ➜ **A ~30% gap between the house model and the company's own guided framework is the single biggest number on this page and it is the whole setup: JPM expects management *"to either raise or provide stronger qualitative support"* for FY27 AI revenue at the print.** **Drivers named: expanding ASIC/XPU ramps across **GOOGL, [[META]], OAI, [[ANTHROPIC]], ByteDance/Alibaba**, plus AI networking on the **Tomahawk 6 (102T)** cycle. Near-term: July-quarter strength on the **initial TPU v8i ramp**, continued **TPU v7**, the **initial ramp of Meta's MTIA "Iris" ASIC**, and Tomahawk 5 — with October guidance implying **FY26 AI revenue >$56B** and the **initial volume ramp of [[OPENAI]]'s "Jalapeno" 1st-gen XPU ASIC**.**
🔴 **THE SUPPLY DATAPOINT THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FORECAST: *"**Tomahawk 5 demand still EXCEEDING SUPPLY** and next-gen **Tomahawk 6 effectively SOLD OUT THROUGH NEXT YEAR**."*** ➜ **A sold-out networking book through CY27 is a harder constraint-and-visibility statement than any revenue framework, and it is the piece of the FY27 call that does not depend on hyperscaler ramp timing.**
**FY28, framed as commentary rather than a target: JPM does *"not expect management to provide a formal FY28 AI revenue target"* but expects strongly constructive tone, supported by **GOOGL TPU v9 2nm ramping early CY28**, **incremental [[ANTHROPIC]] TPU demand**, **META MTIA expansion**, **OAI Jalapeno 1st/2nd-gen ASICs**, and ByteDance/Alibaba.** ⚠️ **Context for the whole note — the reason it exists: *"AVGO shares have LAGGED the broader semiconductor group YTD (SOX +69% vs. AVGO +7%)"*, which JPM attributes to the GOOGL share-loss fear plus the absence of incremental FY27 colour. This is a mean-reversion argument as much as a fundamental one.** |
| 08-21 | 🔴 **Wells Fargo · Aaron Rakers (Technology Weekly)** | capital | **bull** | **TWO SEPARATE AVGO ITEMS, AND THE FINANCING ONE IS THE BIGGER OF THE TWO. (1) *"Broadcom reportedly raising AS MUCH AS $100B OF DEBT for AI financing"* — Rakers frames it as AI-CHIP financing *"that would benefit [[ANTHROPIC]] and other companies"*, i.e. vendor-adjacent financing of the customer's build rather than balance-sheet capex by AVGO itself.** ➤ **This belongs next to every XPU-demand datapoint on this page: if the customer's capacity is being financed through the supplier's balance sheet, the FY27 AI revenue line and the credit line are no longer independent variables. Cross-filed to [[ANTHROPIC]].** **(2) AVGO surfaces as a candidate for [[SKHYNIX]]'s undisclosed "Customer B" (13.4% of SK hynix 2Q26 revenue — larger than Customer A, widely believed to be [[NVDA]]), on the evidence of **$128.1B in purchase commitments**. Rakers does not resolve the identity; the other candidates are [[GOOG]] ($811B of commits), [[AMD]] and [[AAPL]].** ⚠️ **If AVGO were Customer B it would be a direct read on ASIC/XPU HBM consumption running ahead of NVDA's — which is the direction [hbm-memory](themes/hbm-memory.md) already has from the 08-18 customer-level 2028 split. Logged as corroborating but UNCONFIRMED.** (Wells Fargo Securities · Aaron Rakers, Technology Weekly, 2026-08-21) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/Wells_Fargo_Hynix_-_Whos_Customer_B_CPO_Roadmap_S_Korea_Memo.html) |
| **08-25** | 🔴🔴 **SemiAnalysis · Bryan Shan / Myron Xie / Jordan Nanos / Wega Chu / Clara Ee / **Dylan Patel** — "OpenAI Jalapeño: Better Than Nvidia Blackwell?"** (**independent research, NOT a broker — no rating, no PT, no estimate**) | produto / competicao | **bull** | 🔴 **THE FULLEST THIRD-PARTY VALIDATION THE OPENAI XPU PROGRAM HAS EVER HAD, AND AVGO IS NAMED AS THE PARTNER TWICE OVER — silicon *and* switching.** SemiAnalysis was invited into OpenAI's labs and benchmarked the part with its InferenceX suite. **The chip:** unveiled in June *"in partnership with Broadcom, built from a blank slate exclusively for LLM inference"*; design work began mid-2024, **tape-out (of the CoWoS design, not just the top die) in November 2025** — *"an extremely fast ASIC development cycle"* of ~16 months. All published results are on **A0 silicon 9 months into the program**; a **B0 stepping is already in the fab with *"roughly a 25% perf-per-watt improvement"***, delivering **13.4 PFLOPs MXFP4 on a single reticle-sized N3P compute die at 700W TDP** (vs **17.5 PFLOPs of dense NVFP4 for a similar-size Rubin compute die on the same node at 900–1,150W**). **HBM4 at 15.4TB/s per package** — *"implies its HBM4 can hit 10Gbps pin speeds, which would give it a slight edge over the 9.6Gbps Nvidia is getting out of its HBM4 in Rubin"*; *"The HBM is likely provided by [[SAMSUNG]]."* 🔴 **THE AVGO NETWORKING CONTENT, WHICH IS THE PART THIS PAGE HAS BEEN MISSING — the scale-up fabric is Broadcom silicon end to end: each rack carries 8 "Chana" switch trays, *"six … for the local domain, which come with one 102.4T Tomahawk 6 switch ASIC each"*, plus 2 global trays that SemiAnalysis thinks could be *"2x 102.4T Tomahawk 6 switches making up to 204.8T per switch tray"*.** Rack topology: 16 "Vindaloo" trays × 8 ASICs = **128 Jalapeño per rack**, local all-to-all over a copper backplane *"just like that of Nvidia's Oberon"*, global domain of **2,048 XPUs across 16 racks** (copper backplane + 1.6T transceivers + optical circuit switch, 8 rails); **~160kW for the two-rack (host + ASIC) system, "basically a double-wide GB300 rack"**; system-level design *"done in partnership with Celestica"*; host "Katsu" trays run **2× [[AMD]] Turin-class EPYC + 1.5TB DRAM**. **Timing: *"production is currently scheduled to gradually ramp over 2027 with most of the output currently scheduled for Q4 of next year"*; *"The next goal is 100MW."*** ⚠️ **THE OFFSET, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS THE MARGIN ARGUMENT AND IT COMES FROM A BULL: on perf/TCO Jalapeño and Vera Rubin are *"head-to-head, producing almost the same number of output tokens per $"*, and SemiAnalysis attributes part of that to price rather than physics — *"part of this TCO advantage comes from trading Nvidia's high margins for Broadcom's lower (though still high) margins."* Also, sizing the switching opportunity honestly: *"scale-up networking is only about 10% of total system cost."*** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/2026_08_25_ENC_OpenAI_Jalape%C3%B1o_Better_Than_Nvidia_Blackwell_email.html) · [archived (truncated) PDF copy](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/Semianalysis_on_Jalapeno.html) |
| 08-24 | 🔴🔴 **Morgan Stanley Asia-Pacific Technology · Charlie Chan / Daniel Yen / Daisy Dai / Tiffany Yeh (w/ Lucas Wang, Ethan Jia, Henry Zhao) — "AI Supply Chain: Google ASIC, who gets what for design services?"** (**no AVGO rating or PT in this note — AVGO is Joe Moore's coverage in the US**) | competicao / produto | **mixed** | 🔴 **THE ASIA-SIDE TPU LADDER, AND IT SPLITS THE FRANCHISE BY GENERATION RATHER THAN TAKING IT AWAY. MS Asia is explicit that AVGO is NOT being displaced on the core: *"We would not yet assume it is displacing the existing TPU designers (Broadcom in the US … and MediaTek in Taiwan)"*, and on [[MRVL]] specifically — *"Marvell is winning additional content around TPU rather than replacing core TPU itself."*** MS Asia TPU shipment forecast (k units, Exhibit 3): **v7 Ironwood (AVGO) 300 '25 / 2,300 '26 / 200 '27 · v8i Sunfish (3nm, AVGO) 900 '26 / 4,000 '27 / 2,500 '28 · v8t Zebrafish (3nm, MediaTek) 500 '26 / 3,000 '27 / 1,000 '28 · v9 Humufish (2nm, MediaTek) 150 '27 / 3,000 '28 · v10 Icefish (1.4nm, MediaTek) and v10a Merope (2nm, "by US design service") both "unknown"**; total **3,700 '26 → 7,350 '27 → >6,500 '28**. ⚠️ **The uncomfortable read is in the same exhibit: every generation from v8t forward is attributed to MediaTek, whose TPU revenue MS Asia models at US$13.5bn '27 → $43.5bn '28 → ~$70bn '29 (65% of MediaTek's 2028 revenue) at a US$18k v10 ASP recognised by MediaTek — so "not displaced" is a statement about the CORE COMPUTE DIE, not about generational share.** Three trends named for v10: **KGD (Google owns the compute-die design and the photomask), 3.5D packaging, and a reticle size expanding to >12 reticles from ~9** — and *"We think TPU v10 continues to use [[INTC]] EMIB-T given the larger reticle size."* MS Asia also relays that **[[AMD]] "may also be involved in TPUv10"** (sourced to Joe Moore's 08-19 MRVL note). ⚠️ **INTERNAL INCONSISTENCY IN THE NOTE, LOGGED AND DELIBERATELY NOT RESOLVED: Exhibit 2 labels TPU v10 as "Sunfish"; Exhibit 3 labels v8i as Sunfish and v10 as Icefish. The Exhibit-3 naming is the one consistent with everything else on this page (JPM 08-20, @sssjeffpu 08-24) and is the one used above.** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/GLOBAL_20260824_1435.html) |
| 08-24 | 🔴 **MS Asia · Chan et al. — same note, COWOS ALLOCATION EXHIBITS (13–15)** | supply | **mixed** | **2027e CoWoS allocation, k wafers (share of total): [[NVDA]] 1,222 (45%) · [[AMD]] 530 (20%) · BROADCOM 484 (18%) · MediaTek 180 (7%) · AWS/Annapurna 90 · [[MRVL]] 64 (2%) · GUC 60 (2%) · AWS/Alchip 36 · Xilinx 10 · Cisco 6 · others 12 — total 2,694k.** 2026e for contrast: **NVDA 780 (56%) · Broadcom 300 (22%) · AMD 130 (9%) · MediaTek 40 (3%) · MRVL 17 (1%) — total 1,394k.** Y/Y growth into 2027: **Broadcom +61% · NVDA +57% · AMD +308% · MRVL +276%.** ⚠️ **This does NOT supersede the Fubon mark already on this page (07-28: 22.3% / 421,800 wafers for 2027). Different house, different total base (MS 2,694k vs Fubon's implied ~1.89m) — so AVGO's WAFER count is HIGHER at MS while its SHARE is LOWER. Both marks are kept. What the two houses agree on is the direction: AVGO's share of CoWoS falls in 2027 while AMD's rises sharply.** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/GLOBAL_20260824_1435.html) |
| 08-24 | **MS Asia · Chan et al. — same note, 2027 WAFER + HBM TAM (Exhibits 11–12)** | supply / margem | **bull** | The bottom-up TAM under AVGO's two named programs: **TPU v8i (Sunfish) — 330k CoWoS wafers at 12 chips/wafer ⇒ 3,960k chips; 800mm² 3nm compute die ×2 ⇒ ~296k wafers consumed ⇒ US$8.08bn of 2027 wafer TAM; 288GB of HBM3e 12-hi (Samsung/Hynix/Micron).** **TPU v7p (Ironwood) — 13k wafers ⇒ 208k chips; 192GB HBM3e 8-hi; US$0.37bn.** Industry totals: **2027 AI wafer TAM at least US$59bn (vs at least US$26bn in 2026); AI HBM demand up to ~48.6bn Gb.** ➜ **Useful as an independent cross-check on the HBM-pass-through margin drag this page tracks: MS Asia still has AVGO's 2027 volume node on HBM3e 12-hi while [[NVDA]] Rubin moves to HBM4 — a cost-basis difference, not only a content one.** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/GLOBAL_20260824_1435.html) |
| 08-24 | **Mizuho · Vijay Rakesh (w/ David Liu, Jacob Grandstaff, Rafal Tworkowski) — "SemiAnalysis: Memory De-Spec, WFE, 800V Uncertainty with Copper/Optics Upside"; OUTPERFORM, PT $530 UNCHANGED** (px $358.76) ⚠️ **THIS IS A MIZUHO NOTE REPORTING A DINNER WITH SEMIANALYSIS PRESIDENT DOUG O'LAUGHLIN — it is NOT a SemiAnalysis publication. The ingest router mis-detected the broker off the title; corrected here.** | supply / demanda | **bull** | 🔴 **THE READ-ACROSS THAT MATTERS FOR AN ASIC VENDOR: memory de-spec is framed as a VOLUME UNLOCK, not a demand signal. SA argues GPU/ASIC OEMs *"could cap future HBM content at 4-8Hi with 12Hi less appealing"*, that the HBM *"trade ratio 3:1 to 5:1 DRAM"* constraint is diminishing, and Mizuho concludes *"the lower DRAM/GPU content could allow ~20-30% more GPU/ASIC shipments"* — driving *"higher aggregate DRAM bit demand for Training/Inference, KVCache"* **and better GPU/ASIC margins.*** Mizuho's own supply check on AVGO's volume node: **"[[GOOG]] has also scaled down TPUv8 HBM3e to 8Hi."** AVGO thesis marks restated in the same note: **GM/OM ~77%/67%, FCF/yr growing toward ~$52bn by F26E, AI revenue to ~$157bn by F28E (98% 3Y CAGR), 2025 custom-silicon share ~70-80% of a market growing to $150-170bn by 2028E**; PT basis **~25x C27E EPS of $21.17** (bull $700 at ~30x $23.28; bear $400 at ~20x $19.60). [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/SemiAnalysis__Memory_De-Spec_WFE_800V_Uncertainty_with_Cop.html) |
| 08-05 | 🔴 **Morgan Stanley TMT **CREDIT** Research · Lindsay A Tyler — "AVGO Credit: TPUs, Take Two?"** ⚠️ **CREDIT RESEARCH — no equity rating, no equity PT, and MS discloses it is acting as advisor to Broadcom on the AI XPV Platform and is paid fees for it.** | capital | **mixed** | 🔴 **THE SECOND TRANCHE, AND THE NUMBER IT IMPLIES.** Bloomberg reported 08-04 that Blackstone *"has held early discussions to gauge interest in a second TPU-financing tranche for an unrated AI lab, similar or greater in size relative to the ~$35bn first tranche"* (MS is *"not aware of a deal"*; representatives declined to comment). MS's read: since the first tranche was described as scalable, a second could also carry residual-value support — **"If a second tranche and the remaining implied ~17GW platform resemble the first tranche, AVGO's theoretical maximum supported exposure could surpass ~$300bn"** (the earlier ~$480bn high end *"now appears punitive following APCHIP amortization disclosures"*). **Cash spread marks, 08-04: AVGO 5.700% '56 at +110 (MTD −19bp, QTD +29bp) · AVGO 4.950% '36 at +95 (−12 / +23) · [[NVDA]] 5.625% '56 at +99 · NVDA 4.950% '36 at +76 · [[INTC]] 6.125% '56 at +125 · INTC 5.300% '36 at +95.** MS: AVGO 10Y has widened **~25bp since June-end** and is *"now in-line with the BBB index, which we still do not view as a compelling re-entry point"*, with **Aa1/AA [[NVDA]] trading only ~20bp tighter** — but AVGO's **~20-30bp pickup to ~AA hyperscalers *"limits the case for an outright short."*** 🔴 **The industry number, and it is the largest on this page: AVGO's AI XPV alone at 20GW could drive *"an additional ~$430-550bn of TPU/XPU financing beyond the first tranche"*; combined with the WSJ-reported ~$350bn of [[NVDA]] GPU financing plus a ~$250bn lease backstop for an unrated lab and a 10GW Ohio project, chip-financing needs for two labs could *"surpass ~$600bn by decade-end, far exceeding the compute-contract-backed DDTL market we see today."*** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/TMTCREDIT_20260805_0228.html) |
| 06-19 | 🔴 **MS TMT **CREDIT** Research · Lindsay A Tyler / Nishant Satyam — "AVGO Credit: More Compute, More Contingency?"** ⚠️ **CREDIT RESEARCH, HISTORICAL BACKFILL (2026-06-19). No equity rating or PT — this is a BOND recommendation change and must never be read as an equity downgrade.** | capital | **bear (credit)** | **MS CLOSED its buy recommendations on AVGO 33s-36s** — including positions opened **12/19/23 at +116/+119bp** — *"as we see risk/reward as now balanced, with the new overhang offsetting strong growth, technicals, and mitigants at current levels"*; explicitly *"we are not turning negative"*, and *"strong technicals (recent tender, path to net cash) argue against a short."* 🔴 **THE ILLUSTRATIVE EXPOSURE MATH THAT PRODUCED THE HEADLINE FIGURES THIS PAGE NOW CARRIES: the $35bn deal / $29bn max RVS supports 1.2–1.5GW ⇒ implied financing of $23.3–29.2bn per GW and max RVS exposure of $19.3–24.2bn per GW ⇒ scaled to a 15–20GW platform, ~$290–480bn of theoretical maximum RVS exposure** (MS headline ~$350bn). **Rating-agency treatment, all dated 6/11/26: Moody's could incorporate the economic liability into adjusted debt if lessee credit quality weakens — but believes robust demand makes the trigger sequence unlikely over 2-3 years — while warning the *"primary risk lies in multiple such transactions occurring over a short period"*; S&P calls the RVS *"a contingent debt-like obligation"*, intends to include it in adjusted debt over the life of the deal (rising in early years as hardware is delivered, falling in outer years as tenant leases pay down), and will monitor the *"appropriateness"* of its positive outlook; Fitch will adjust debt but *"meaningfully below"* the $29bn cap.** **Tail-risk leverage run with $385bn of RVS treated as debt: gross adjusted leverage 1.4x FY26E → 2.5x FY27E → 3.3x FY28E versus Moody's 2.5x gross downgrade threshold; net adjusted 0.9x / 1.5x / 1.8x versus S&P's 3x net — so *"the issue therefore would more be a hypothetical capital call, rather than the ratings impact."*** ⚠️ **The equity model underneath is Joe Moore's dated 6/4/26 and is therefore STALE against the marks at the top of this page — carried for its SHAPE, not its level: AI semi rev $30.9bn LTM → $56.4bn FY26E → $118.1bn FY27E → $164.3bn FY28E; total rev $75.5 → $105.7 → $167.5 → $214.3bn; GAAP EBITDA $41.5 → $62.9 → $101.4 → $129.6bn; net debt $45.3bn today → NET CASH $49.4bn FY27E and $147.8bn FY28E.** 🔴 **The single sharpest structural line, and the one this page's residual-value risk actually turns on: the tail risk is *"heightened by the lack of an established TPU/XPU secondary market. TPUs appear less fungible and liquid than GPUs, with third-party sales only recently disclosed by [[GOOG]]."*** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/TMTCREDIT_20260619_1518.html) |
