| 08-09 | **Mizuho · Vijay Rakesh "AVGO: Significant TPU Scaling With CoPoS/EMIB — Upside OAI, META, ARM" — ESTIMATES CHANGE, reiterate Outperform PT $530 (now 23x C27E EPS, prior 25x; px $427.76)** | estimativas / competicao | **bull** | 🔴 **The first sell-side estimate RAISE on the FY28 step-up since the Samsung MOU, and it goes well above Street: F27E $169B/$19.49 → $184B/$21.27 (cons $174B/$19.28, EPS +9.2% vs old, +10.3% vs cons); F28E $223B/$25.88 → $261B/$30.65 (cons $228B/$25.69, +18.4% vs old, +19.3% vs cons). F27/28E AI revs $125B/$201B (cons $120B/$165B); calendar-year AI revs $141B (C27E) / $225B (C28E)** — the C28 mark sits mid-range of this page's step-up marks (Arete $177B < Mizuho $225B < house $274B < JPM $250-300B < Barclays >$300B). JulQ and F26E untouched ($29.4B/$3.20; $106B/$11.67). GM held at **75.5%/75.2% F27/28E** vs cons 73.0%/72.4% — an implicit rebuttal of the ASIC-mix GM-compression bear. **The unit model behind it (Ex-1, AVGO ASIC units by customer): total 6.82M ('26E) → 11.42M ('27E) → 18.38M ('28E) → 28.88M ('29E), of which Google 2.73/4.14/5.93/8.45M and — the page-relevant novelty — [[ANTHROPIC]] as AVGO's #2 ASIC customer at 2.08M ('26E) → 3.60M ('27E) → 4.99M ('28E) → 7.80M ('29E)** ("TPU shipments to Anthropic could accelerate through 2029E," higher-ASP training ASICs gaining share), plus Meta 0.59→2.02M, OpenAI 0.26→1.81M, Apple 86k→551k, and **first-time 2028E line items for Oracle (184k), xAI (280k) and [[ARM]] (682k, ASIC "potentially SOP 2027E")**. GOOGL+Anthropic = **~$96B/~$126B of C27/28E revs**. ⚠️ **Basis care: Mizuho counts Anthropic's TPUs separately from Google's** (Ex-8 labels it "Anthropic (TPU ex-GOOGL)"), so its 6.8M '26E AVGO-total is NOT comparable to UBS's 4.8M "total TPU" (07-02) or Fubon's ~3.06M TPU-only ('26). **On the Google share war this page tracks, Mizuho takes the AVGO side of the training/inference split: AVGO keeps the v8t TRAINING chip** ("most-capable TH6/Jericho SerDes/Switch for all-to-all 1000-1M XPU networking at 200G/lane… 2-3x ASP ~$15K/ASIC"), **[[MEDIATEK]] "could potentially win the TPU inference chip, DESPITE investors stating MTK is getting the training chip with large die on EMIB"** — Mizuho thinks the training die is "challenging with a lack of strong SerDes" for MTK (needs GOOGL/SMTC contribution, "potential delays"); v9/v10 = "a mix of both MTK/AVGO"; **MTK stays <10% of the AI ASIC market** (units 520k/2.8M/4.0M/4.5M '26-'29E). ⚠️ **Intra-Mizuho margin fork logged, mirroring the intra-UBS Arcuri/Lin tension already on this page: Rakesh (US) sees MTK's BILLABLE contribution at 20-25% GM ("actual billable contribution from MTK potentially small SerDes") vs his own Mizuho-Asia colleague Kevin Wang at ~40-50% — vs AVGO ~75%.** **The 2028 TPU frame: base 20M+ GOOGL TPUs (50/50 training/inference), "more realistically 20-25M" w/ 3rd-party expansion (Fluidstack et al), blue-sky 35M ("we believe internal GOOGL targets") — AVGO ~50% share = 10-12M units, 3x today's ~4.5M, ">2x added upside" to Mizuho's own 2028E AI revs if 35M lands.** Read against the page's checks: Jefferies/Fubon 12-15M Google TPUs 2028 (chip basis), MS ~10GW+/~9GW-TPU. **The gating item is PACKAGING, which is the note's actual thesis: TPU ramp acceleration "depends on CoPoS and EMIB-T availability by 2028-29E."** TSMC CoWoS to **140kwpm YE26E (prior 120k) and >200kwpm YE27E (prior 170k)**; **CoPoS = ~5x CoWoS surface area, 9.5x→14x reticle by 2029E (Rubin CoWoS-L ~5.5x today), transition POTENTIALLY PULLED IN to 2H27E-1H28E (vs 2H28E-29E)** on panel-warpage/TGV fixes → "5-10x ASIC/GPU output"; **[[INTC]] EMIB-T at 10-12x reticle with Ibiden as key partner → "~3M TPU units based on EMIB-T starting in 2028E"**; TSMC SoW-X 2027E at ~40x reticle; power CoWoS 0.56pJ/bit vs EMIB ~0.30pJ/bit (~50% saving; CoPoS "20pj/bit" as printed — likely 0.20). ⚠️ **One number NOT to take at face value: Mizuho cites GOOGL purchase commitments "expanding significantly to $800B+ FOR TPUs (10Q, p55)" with "NTM TPU delivery estimated at $200B" — this page already caught Barclays over-reading the same disclosure (cited $811B vs the filing's $707.0B; house number $707.0B, and the filing's purchase-commitment line is not TPU-only). Second broker to inflate the same 10-Q figure; keep $707.0B.** Comp frame: AVGO 20x/14x F27/28E EPS vs AMD 33x / MTK 28x / MRVL 36x (C27E P/E 20.8x, PEG 0.4x); AI-rev league table '26-'28E: NVDA $367.6/519.9/645.1B, AVGO $72/141/225B, AMD $11/40.2/60.2B, MTK $2/16.3/42.6B, [[MRVL]] $6.1/9.2/14.4B (MRVL ASIC units held flat at 480k '27-'29E — see MRVL.md; AlChip framed as the Trainium-share gainer). Token-cost exhibit → [themes/tokenmaxxing](themes/tokenmaxxing.md). (Mizuho Securities USA · Vijay Rakesh / David Liu / Jacob Grandstaff / Rafal Tworkowski, 2026-08-09) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/e17e9353-a71e-4d52-9633-40b447056486.html) |
| 08-09 | **JPM "Global Memory TAM and S&D update" (Jay Kwon / Sangsik Lee / Neelay Kamath / Harlan Sur / Mio Shikanai) — the [[SAMSUNG]] >$200bn LTA gets an allocation split (90-95% HBM-for-ASIC), and the memory model says ASIC passes NVDA as the biggest HBM bit consumer in 2027E** | supply / demanda | **bull** | ★ **THE LTA IS GIVEN A COMPOSITION FOR THE FIRST TIME.** JPM: *"SEC announced a **>$200bn LTA contract with AVGO**, in which we expect the **majority of the contract value (90-95%) allocated to HBM for ASIC** (likely TPU and other ASIC projects including OpenAI/Anthropic)."* ⚠️ **Two guardrails, both material: the 90-95% split is JPM's modelled allocation, and the customer names carry JPM's own hedge ("likely"). Neither is disclosed — do not carry either as a company datapoint.** What it does establish is direction: JPM expects Samsung to *"gain M/S in BOTH NVDA and ASIC segments"* off the back of it, and Samsung is already **the dominant HBM supplier into the ASIC complex (52-54% wallet share '26-27E vs SKH 34-38%, MU 10-12%)** — i.e. **AVGO's ASIC programs are being memory-secured through Samsung specifically**, which is a supply-continuity positive for the 2027-28 ramps this page tracks. ★ **THE POOL-SIZE POINT: HBM bit demand mix goes ASIC 22% ('25) → 35% ('26E) → 48% ('27E) → 46% ('28E) vs NVDA 69% → 59% → 42% → 45% — the custom-ASIC complex overtakes NVIDIA as the largest consumer of HBM bits in 2027E.** Independent corroboration of the ASIC ramp from a memory S&D model rather than an accelerator model — though note **~half the crossover is the NVDA Rubin/Rubin Ultra de-spec** (NVDA HBM bits cut 7% / 31% / 40%) rather than ASIC upside, so it overstates relative ASIC strength if read alone. Cross-refs [custom-asic-tpu](themes/custom-asic-tpu.md), [hbm-memory](themes/hbm-memory.md). ⚠️ **Basis note vs the Mizuho row above:** Mizuho's AVGO unit model and JPM's HBM bit model are built on different denominators (accelerator units vs 1GB-equivalent memory bits) and are **not additive or directly comparable** — held side by side. (JPM · Jay Kwon et al., 2026-08-09) |
| 08-07 | **Jefferies Semis EPS Debrief Call · Blayne Curtis — the defence is intact but PUSHED OUT TO THE FALL, and asked to choose between AVGO and [[NVDA]] into year-end he declines both** | valuation / guidance | **mixed** | **Asked directly "from here into year end, like Broadcom or Nvidia, which one do you like more?": "WE DEFENDED BROADCOM AND SAID THAT HOPEFULLY IT WORKS IN THE FALL. I DON'T KNOW THROUGH THIS EARNINGS PERIOD IF THERE'S A LOT NEW THAT THEY CAN ADD. THEY'RE KIND OF STUCK WITH THE 10 GIGAWATTS THEY'VE ALREADY PUT OUT FOR THIS YEAR, AND I DON'T THINK THEY'RE GOING TO REALLY CHANGE THAT THAT MUCH. We talked about that THEY'RE WORKING ON SUPPLY FOR '28 AND MAYBE THEY WOULD GET A LITTLE MORE CONFIDENT IN GUIDING '28 — I STILL DON'T THINK IT'S THROUGH THIS EARNINGS [period]. So, TO BE HONEST, I DON'T PARTICULARLY LIKE EITHER THROUGH EARNINGS. Maybe [[NVDA]] given this tailwind can work a bit, but I think IT'LL BE LIMITED. AND IT'S REALLY ABOUT '28 FOR BROADCOM, WHICH I THINK WILL BE MORE OF A FALL OR LATE FALL STORY."** ⚠️ **THE ACTIONABLE PART IS THE TIMING, NOT THE THESIS: the AVGO bull case is intact but explicitly BACK-END LOADED — the catalyst is a FY28 supply/guide disclosure that he does not expect at this print. "Stuck with the 10 gigawatts they've already put out for this year" is a statement that the 2026 disclosure is done and the next re-rating input is the 2028 book. Read against this page's own catalyst list: it argues against paying up into the print and for the fall.** ⚠️ **A COMPETITIVE DATAPOINT FROM THE SAME CALL, on the copper/ACC opportunity at [[GOOG]] that this page tracks: MACOM said "the DESIGNS ARE NOT SETTLED. So they think that the designs WOULD BE SPLIT IF IT HAPPENS, but they're a lot LESS POSITIVE ON THE OPPORTUNITY just because IT HASN'T SETTLED AND THERE'S LOTS OF OPTIONS BEING THROWN OUT THERE."** ⚠️ **Attributed to MACOM via Jefferies, on the ACC/copper-interconnect socket rather than on AVGO's ASIC franchise — logged as adjacent competitive colour, not as an AVGO datapoint.** **Also relevant to the component-bottleneck read-through this wiki carries from the [[AAOI]] call (TIA/DSP constraints naming AVGO among the gating suppliers): Jefferies flags MACOM "moving into CW LASERS" with "a lot going to happen in lasers over the next two years," i.e. more suppliers entering the laser/analog content layer.** ⚠️ **Machine-transcribed call; names normalised. (Jefferies Semis EPS Debrief Call · Blayne Curtis, 2026-08-07)** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/2026_08_07_jefferies_semis_eps_debrief_call.html) |
| **08-04** | 🔴🔴 **Financial Times · Ryan McMorrow (San Francisco) — "Inside Google's $200bn Wall Street finance machine for Anthropic". THE $128BN OF PURCHASE COMMITMENTS ON THIS PAGE GET A CUSTOMER, A PURPOSE AND A DATE — AND THIS ARTICLE PREDATES THE EPOCH AI NOTE BELOW BY EIGHT DAYS** ⚠️ **JOURNALISM, NOT SELL-SIDE RESEARCH: no rating, no PT, no model; sourced to *"people involved in the project and corporate filings reviewed by the FT."*** | **demanda / capital** | **bull (contracted demand) / mixed (customer concentration + vendor credit)** | 🗓️ **DATE ORDER: **FT = 2026-08-04, earlier-dated primary journalism; Epoch AI · Campbell Hutcheson = 2026-08-12, the later analytical write-up** (logged at 08-12 below, folded into Current state on 08-17).** 🔴🔴 **★ THE HEADLINE ITEM FOR THIS PAGE: *"The largest deal yet came together in **APRIL** when [[GOOG]] agreed to sell **ANOTHER 3.5GW of TPU hardware to Broadcom** for deployment by [[ANTHROPIC]]. **Broadcom's filings disclose $128BN of purchase commitments, with $55.2BN of deliveries scheduled for its 2027 fiscal year and $72.9BN for 2028. People familiar with the arrangements said those commitments relate ALMOST ENTIRELY to its agreed purchase of 3.5GW of TPU hardware from Google.**"*** ✅ **Identical to the figures this page carries from Wells Fargo (08-18): $128.1bn exiting F2Q26, $55.2bn FY27 / $72.9bn FY28, "primarily inventory." Wells had the NUMBER; the FT has the CUSTOMER, the PRODUCT, the POWER (3.5GW) and the DATE (April).** ➜ **① Re-labels the line from diversified inventory to a single customer-specific obligation. ② Corroborates the F2Q26 10-Q's "long-term contract (SINGULAR) for custom AI ASICs" that Wells inferred was Google. ③ Makes the FY27/FY28 split a Google-TPU DELIVERY SCHEDULE, to be scored against the TPU GW/unit estimates on this page rather than against total AI revenue. ④ Is the sharpest concentration statistic here: $128bn of Broadcom's own purchase commitments now rest on one end-customer's lease payments.** ⚠️ **UNIT TRAP, FLAGGED IMMEDIATELY: $128bn ÷ 3.5GW ≈ $36.6bn/GW is what Broadcom PAYS GOOGLE for COMPLETE RACK SYSTEMS — not AVGO content per GW. Never compare or chain it with the $10–20bn/GW (Hock's own range) or Jefferies' $11bn/GW on this page. Same discipline already applied to Epoch's $34.5bn ÷ ">1 GW".** 🆕 **NET-NEW #1 — THE GUARANTEE'S PROPER NAME AND ITS RUN-OFF: *"Broadcom, in effect, **guaranteed the two senior tranches** by agreeing to cover any shortfall if Anthropic stopped paying and the SPV could not sell the hardware for enough to make the senior investors whole. The arrangement, known as **RESIDUAL VALUE SUPPORT**, covers about **$30bn of the $35bn financing**, with **Broadcom's exposure declining as Anthropic makes its lease payments**."*** ➜ **Use "residual value support" as the term of art. Combined with Epoch's draw schedule (~16 stages, ~$24bn out by summer 2027), the exposure profile is a HUMP peaking around mid-2027 — building with the draw, running off with the lease — not a plateau at the $29bn cap.** ✅ **Reconciles, does not replace: FT "~$30bn of the $35bn financing" · Epoch "$30bn of the $34.5bn of debt" · AVGO's disclosed "$29bn maximum exposure." One arrangement, three resolutions — $35bn is the SPV's PURCHASE PRICE, $34.5bn the DEBT, $29bn the disclosed CAP.** 🆕 **NET-NEW #2 — WHY BROADCOM IS IN THE MIDDLE AT ALL, quoted: *"none of the companies involved wanted to carry tens of billions of dollars of AI chips on their balance sheets. Google is already funding record capital spending, so **Broadcom agreed to buy the AI hardware from it. But Broadcom, too, would rather deploy its capital elsewhere.** 'Broadcom is the financing provider, but they don't want to be in the financing business.'"* Resolution: *"**Morgan Stanley helped arrange a private-credit vehicle**… in an adaptation of the **vendor-financing model BOEING AND GE built to sell aircraft and engines**."* **The Boeing/GE analogy is the right mental model for this exposure and is net-new here; MS as arranger is also net-new (Epoch named Apollo/Blackstone/global banks as the capital).** FT's risk split: *"Google guarantees the data centres. **Broadcom commits to buying the chips and helps finance them.** Apollo and Blackstone provide much of the private-credit capital."*** 🆕 **NET-NEW #3 — THE FIRST TRANCHE, DATED AND UNIT-COUNTED: *"In **JUNE**, the first tranche of TPU hardware passed **from Google THROUGH Broadcom** into this financing blender. A special-purpose vehicle known as **Compute SPV paid $35bn for roughly 1GW** of the AI hardware, representing **around 1MN TPUs**."* The pass-through routing is the mechanical reason the commitments appear on Broadcom's books.** ⚠️🔴 **QUANTITY DISCIPLINE — FOUR NUMBERS, NONE SUPERSEDING ANOTHER: ① **>$150bn of CHIPS** supplied to Anthropic; ② **~$200bn of CONTRACT NOTIONAL**, *"roughly four-fifths tied to the chips themselves"* (≈$160bn chip leg); ③ **~$50bn of DEBT RAISED** (Epoch, 08-12); ④ **$35bn PAID** for the first ~1GW. **The FT's larger figures do NOT overwrite Epoch's ~$50bn — chips and contracts vs debt.** For this page ①/② are the hardware-value statement (product moving through Broadcom) and ③ is the financing quantum; the $128bn of filed commitments is the audited slice.** **CONTEXT, FOR SYMMETRY: Google's counterpart exposure is also now sized — *"backstopped 10 developments with 2.4GW of power… on the hook for as much as $44bn if all the leases go bad, though it marks the liability at $815mn."* Epoch's conclusion (financing is not the binding constraint PROVIDED suppliers absorb part of the risk) is now quantified on both legs: AVGO ~$29-30bn on chips, GOOG ~$44bn notional on datacentres.** ⚠️ **THE BEAR FRAMING, from the source: *"the risk is big if concentrated: $200bn of contracts tied to Anthropic's ability to pay,"* and Jefferies' Jonathan Petersen — *"There's a whole world that's been built underneath those companies, and if their appetite to invest decreases, all of it sees a slowdown. That's the big macro risk."*** **Background: TPUs are *"AI chips [Google] has co-developed with Broadcom since 2016,"* sold in *"pods"* — *"server racks taller than a person that connect thousands of chips into a single computing system."*** [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/Inside_Googles_200bn_Wall_Street_finance_machine_for_Anthropic.html) |
| 08-03 | **Fubon · Sherman Shang / Arthur (hosted by Jefferies), "industry highlights & checks update" — the PRIMARY call transcript behind the "LESS SO FOR BROADCOM" row below** | supply | **bear** | ⚠️ **Machine transcript (ASR) — names garbled; corrected inline on first use.** **The relay told you Broadcom was on the wrong side of this call. The primary tells you WHERE, and it is narrower and more actionable than the relay implied: it is TEST capacity, not foundry, and it is a PUSH-OUT, not a cut.** 🔴 **Verbatim, and this datapoint is not anywhere on this page: "from our supply chain check, we see Broadcom (ASR: 'Borcom') is less bullish in expanding capacity, less bullish in expanding capacity. So Broadcom previously, they plan to add more capacity in the second half. But it seems like the plan is pushed out."** Pressed by an analyst on whether that reflects a Samsung 2nm/advanced-packaging dual-source rather than weak demand, Shang **narrows it himself: "when I said Broadcom is becoming less bullish in expanding capacity, I was talking about THE TESTING CAPACITY. I was talking about the testing capacity. But from the foundry side, I think Broadcom will still use TSMC."** ⚠️ **Two things follow. (a) This is a check-based read on a capacity decision, not a statement about orders — but test capacity is the last thing you add before you ship, so a 2H26 test-capacity push-out is the earliest hard-asset tell the supply chain gives on a 2027 volume expectation. Treat any AVGO-linked test-house guide as at risk (cf. [[NVDA]]'s KYEC guide-down on the same call, from the same mechanism running one generation ahead). (b) It also CUTS AGAINST the page's own 07-28 [[KLAC]]-call datapoint that Broadcom is being qualified at [[SAMSUNG]] Foundry 2nm/3nm — Fubon says "I'm not so sure on that" and keeps Broadcom at TSMC on the foundry side. Both are second-hand; hold them as an open disagreement, not a resolution.** The foundry backdrop he does commit to is a constraint either way: **TSMC 2nm and 3nm supply "will be very, very tight" in 2027**, with TSMC capex rising to **$80-85bn in 2027 from $60-64bn in 2026** and Shang explicitly unsure that is enough — "I'm not sure that is enough to support all the customers demand." 🔴 **On V10, the asymmetry of information is itself the datapoint: "currently, we haven't seen MediaTek's ([[MEDIATEK]]) V10 design. But from our checks, we have already seen Broadcom's V10 design. And it is a gigantic chip with a 8 [2]nm compute dies (ASR: 'a 8 to nanometer compute dies' — read as eight 2nm compute dies, consistent with this page's existing '8 N2 compute dies' mark). And we also heard the TDP of this chip will become 6,000 WATTS, 6,000 watts. And it will also adopt 400 or 448G SerDes (ASR: 'services')."** **The 6,000W TDP is net-new to this page** and is a striking number in its own right — for scale, this page's own AMD Helios read has a whole 72-GPU rack at ~240kW, so a single 6kW accelerator package implies rack- and cooling-level co-design well beyond the current generation. **On SerDes the primary forces a distinction this page must keep clean: Fubon says "Broadcom's 400G is ready" TODAY — which was the explicit basis of his PRIOR view that "in the Google TPU V10, [MediaTek] will probably lose some market share back to Broadcom." That view is now WEAKENED, not by anything Broadcom did, but by MediaTek's earnings-call statement that its own 400G-or-higher SerDes will be ready in 2H27 ("a huge surprise to us"), one year earlier than Fubon assumed — see [MEDIATEK.md](MEDIATEK.md).** ⚠️ **Do NOT net Fubon's "Broadcom's 400G is ready" against this page's standing datapoint that AVGO's **448G** SerDes missed the v9 production deadline (MediaTek delivered 336G). Those are different speed grades and the page should carry both: 400G ready, 448G late.** **Finally, the 2028 expectations bar and the bear question, with Fubon's answer — which declines the bull case as much as the bear: asked about broker 2028 custom-ASIC revenue estimates "ranging somewhere between 35 billion to 52 billion" and whether business could "go back to Broadcom, or indeed Google could change the business model and go like the Alchip or GUC route and do it themselves," Shang's answer is neither: "I think INTEL'S PRODUCTION IS THE BIGGEST RISK… if Intel is unable to enter production, that means MediaTek, they need to go back to TSMC. And I don't think TSMC has the capacity to support Google to do 12 million to 15 million. So when that happens, we will probably see some downside."** ⚠️ **Read that carefully as an AVGO holder: the mechanism by which the $35-52bn 2028 numbers get cut is a TSMC capacity wall, not a reallocation of sockets back to Broadcom. Fubon is not offering AVGO share recapture as the offset — the offset he describes is the whole 2028 TPU number coming down. Vendor-reallocation is explicitly not his swing factor.** Reconfirmed without change: **Tomahawk 6 as one of ASE's three named programs** behind the FoCoS raise to 50k wpm (with [[NVDA]] Vera and [[AMD]] Venice). Google-side TPU volumes/targets on [GOOG.md](GOOG.md); MediaTek share/ASP on [MEDIATEK.md](MEDIATEK.md); Intel EMIB yield/capacity on [INTC.md](INTC.md); TSMC capacity on [TSM.md](TSM.md). (Fubon · Sherman Shang / Arthur (hosted by Jefferies), "industry highlights & checks update", 2026-08-03) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/2026_08_03_sherman_update_03_08_26.html) |
| 08-03 | **Jefferies/Fubon group call (Sherman Shang via Conor O'Mara) — "LESS SO FOR BROADCOM"** | competicao | **bear** | ⚠️ **THE DESK'S OWN ONE-LINE SUMMARY OF THE CALL PUTS AVGO ON THE WRONG SIDE OF IT: "Call with Sherman & Arthur on AI sounds PRETTY BULLISH FOR MEDIATEK & INTEL, LESS SO FOR BROADCOM" (Conor O'Mara, 2026-08-03), and Beavington's version — "Our focus was MEDIATEK (+ve) + INTEL EMIB (+ve) + GOOGLE TPU (+ve) + NVIDIA (−ve)" — simply omits Broadcom from the positives.** **The mechanism is the custom-ASIC share table: Fubon now models [[MEDIATEK]] at 17-18% custom-ASIC share in 2027 rising to 30-32% in 2028, EXCLUDING a potential second accelerator project — against the 07-28 primary note that already trimmed AVGO's TSMC CoWoS allocation to 22.3% / 421,800 wafers in 2027 (from 24.3%) while MediaTek's rose to 7.3% / 137,700. MediaTek's 2027 allocation is now flagged for FURTHER UPSIDE.** ⚠️ **Where AVGO does appear on the positive side, it is as a networking/CPU customer rather than as the ASIC incumbent: ASE's FoCoS capacity was revised UP to 50kwpm specifically to support "NVDA Vera, AMD Venice, AVGO TOMAHAWK 6." Read that as the switch franchise holding up while the accelerator share erodes — two different businesses inside the same ticker.** **Cost item: Fubon puts HBM4 at US$35-36/GB for "other GPU and custom-ASIC projects" versus US$31-32/GB for [[NVDA]] — a ~10-15% BOM disadvantage for Broadcom's ASIC customers against merchant GPU, and "the HBM cost will DOUBLE in 2027."** On the other side of the ledger, from the corpus: **"$AVGO well positioned as it fuels the most ambitious AI companies ([[GOOG]], [[ANTHROPIC]], [[OPENAI]], [[META]])" (@SouthernValue95, 2026-08-03).** (Jefferies/Fubon · Sherman Shang, 2026-08-03) |
| 08-03 | @zerohedge (relaying GS) | valuation | **bear** | **GOLDMAN REMOVED AVGO FROM ITS US CONVICTION LIST: "Goldman changes to its US Conviction Buy list: We ADD Applied Materials ([[AMAT]]), Delta Airlines, MICROSOFT ([[MSFT]]), O'Reilly, Viking Holdings and UPS to the US Conviction List, while REMOVING BROADCOM (AVGO), Dick's Sporting Goods, Johnson & Johnson, and ServiceNow ([[NOW]])"** (@zerohedge, 2026-08-03). ⚠️ **A conviction-list removal is not a rating change and the underlying rating is not stated in the relay — but the substitution pattern is informative: GS rotated conviction from the custom-ASIC merchant toward the semicap equipment supplier and the hyperscaler on the same day. Verify against the GS "US Conviction List — Directors' Cut: August 2026 Update" (Steven Kron, 2026-08-03) before treating it as a rating action.** |
| 08-02 | **J.P. Morgan · Gokul Hariharan — the GUC/MediaTek upgrades name Broadcom's business model as the thing being dismantled** | competicao | **bear** | ⚠️ **★ THE MOST DIRECT STRUCTURAL ATTACK ON AVGO'S ASIC ECONOMICS PUBLISHED THIS QUARTER, and it is stated as a description of [[GOOG]]'s procurement strategy rather than as a Broadcom call: "we believe that through the evolution from TPUv8 to v10 families, THE BLACK-BOX MODEL FROM US FABLESS VENDOR (WHERE IP, DESIGN SERVICE, MANUFACTURING AND PACKAGING VALUE ADD ARE ALL BUNDLED TOGETHER AT HIGH MARGINS) WILL BE BROKEN DOWN SYSTEMATICALLY IN FAVOR OF A DISAGGREGATED APPROACH"** (JPM, GUC upgrade note, 2026-08-02). **"US fabless vendor" is Broadcom, and "bundled together at high margins" is the AVGO ASIC gross margin. The mechanism JPM describes is Google moving to Customer-Owned Tooling: three engagement models are live under the TPU v10 RFQ — (i) semi-CoT with [[MEDIATEK]] supplying SerDes/D2D/packaging/integration; (ii) compute-die back-end outsourced to pure design-service vendors (GUC, Alchip) on Turnkey-3; (iii) ⚠️ the full break-up, where "ONLY IP BEING SOURCED BY GOOGLE SEPARATELY… WITH FOUNDRY PROCUREMENT BEING HANDLED DIRECTLY BY GOOGLE, SIMILAR TO THE AMAZON TRAINIUM APPROACH."** ⚠️ **Model (iii) is the one that matters for this page: if the customer buys its own wafers from [[TSM]] and its own HBM, the ASIC vendor's revenue line collapses toward IP + services and the manufacturing/packaging margin accrues to the customer. Google ALREADY buys its HBM directly (confirmed on MediaTek's 07-31 call), and JPM notes the full-CoT opportunity "could be EVEN HIGHER" for non-TPU projects "given lower design complexity and IP dependence, AS ALREADY ILLUSTRATED FOR THE GOOGLE AXION CPUs" — i.e. the model has already been executed once at this customer, on CPUs.** **THE COMPETITIVE CLAIM ON v10 SPECIFICALLY: JPM says MediaTek's semi-CoT proposal "appears to offer an ATTRACTIVE TCO ADVANTAGE VERSUS BROADCOM, pointing to a high likelihood of securing the project and even potential share expansion," with the award expected in the coming months and mass production late 2029. Nomura the same day put GUC's chairlady on record as "NOT DENYING the company's opportunity in Google's TPU (which from our industry survey MIGHT BE TPU v.10)."** ⚠️ **How to hold this: it is a sell-side supply-chain read from analysts who cover the Asian challengers and therefore have a directional interest, the RFQ is unresolved, and Broadcom's own share of the current TPU generations is not disputed by any of it. But the argument is no longer "MediaTek is taking share at the margin" — it is "the buyer is re-architecting the contract so that no single vendor can hold that margin again." That is a different and more durable risk, and this page should carry it as the primary bear case on the ASIC franchise's terminal margin, cross-filed to [[GOOG]], [[MEDIATEK]], [[TSM]] and [themes/custom-asic-tpu](themes/custom-asic-tpu.md).** **One counterweight from the same house's own hierarchy: JPM expects "the highest value capture… in the next 3-4 years" to accrue to MediaTek with GUC/Alchip benefiting only "somewhat" — so the disaggregation is not yet a diffusion of the margin to everyone, it is a transfer of it to one named competitor.** Separately, corroborating a standing bear item on this page: **[[ANET]] checks say "supply has NOT improved, AVGO SILICON LEAD TIMES STILL >52 WEEKS"** (UBS, 08-01, already logged) — the franchise is supply-constrained at the same moment its largest ASIC customer is engineering around it. |
| 07-28 | **[[KLAC]] Q4 FY26 earnings call (read-through — Broadcom named at [[SAMSUNG]] Foundry 2nm/3nm)** | supply | mixed | **A foundry-diversification datapoint for Broadcom, surfaced second-hand on a semicap call.** JPMorgan's Harlan Sur, listing evidence of leading-edge broadening to KLA: *"**Samsung just announced foundry engagements with new customers, like Broadcom, for example, for 2-nanometer and 3-nanometer**."* ⚠️ **This is an analyst's restatement of prior announcements on a third party's call — not an AVGO, Samsung or KLA disclosure — and should be verified against the primary source before it carries weight in the model.** **Why log it here at all:** the XPU/ASIC franchise's dependence on a single leading-edge foundry is a standing structural question on this page, and a live Samsung 2nm/3nm engagement is the cheapest available hedge against [[TSM]] allocation risk in the CoWoS-constrained window. KLA's CEO endorsed the general dynamic on the call — *"the world needs more advanced logic… a desire by many customers to broaden the other players outside of the leader… and there's been some success in terms of their ability to bring technology online"* — which is a supplier confirming that second-source qualification at the leading edge is actually converting, not just being discussed. ⚠️ Neither volumes, timing, nor which products are implicated is disclosed anywhere in the transcript; do not infer a share shift from this. (KLA Corp Q4 FY26 earnings call — Bloomberg FINAL TRANSCRIPT, 2026-07-28) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/20260728_KLA_Corp-_Earnings_Call_2026-7-28_RT000000003114277744pdf.html) |
| 07-27 | **Morgan Stanley · Joseph Moore (via "Playing the AI Infrastructure Dip", Byrd et al)** | competicao | **bull** | **PT $502 (31% upside at the time), 28x 2027e ModelWare base EPS of $17.92.** The note names the overhang and then dismisses it: AVGO "has the **second largest AI exposure (in absolute dollars) in our coverage** and is poised to grow as hyperscale capex increases. While there has been an investor preference for growthier 'bottleneck' stories across the AI semiconductor ecosystem and **the debate around [[MEDIATEK]] versus Broadcom share on Google TPU is an overhang**, we believe in the continued strength of the company's AI growth trajectory and that **AVGO should remain the majority TPU supplier over time, with ~80% share.**" ⚠️ **Cross-check against the Fubon CoWoS row below, which is the harder supply-side read on the same debate: Fubon has MediaTek's CoWoS allocation jumping 1.5% → 7.3% (17,400 → 137,700 wafers) in 2027 on the TPU program while AVGO holds 22.3% (421,800). MS's ~80% share claim and Fubon's wafer split are not directly comparable (value vs wafers, and TPU-only vs all-XPU) but they point the same way on direction and disagree on magnitude — keep both.** (Morgan Stanley "Playing the AI Infrastructure Dip", 2026-07-27) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/Morgan_Stanley_700HK_Global_Thematics_Playing_the_AI_Infrastructure_Dip_Whe.html) |
| 07-28 | **Fubon Research · Sherman Shang / Daniel Yang — "Our advanced packaging, CoWoS forecast adjustments; other updates"** (dist. Jefferies; sector Overweight) | supply | **mixed** | 🔴 **Fubon's CoWoS model refresh trims AVGO's 2027 TSMC allocation again — and the wafers go to the two names AVGO competes with. Figure 5 has AVGO at 16.2% (2025) → 22.5% (2026) → 22.3% (2027) of TSMC CoWoS = 109,650 → 263,100 → 421,800 wafers.** The 22.3% supersedes Fubon's own **24-25% / ≈459.6k** mark from 06-05 (moved to Changelog). **Read the two halves separately, because they say different things.** **The volume half is strongly positive and this page has been under-weighting it: on a pool growing 675k → 1,170k → 1,890k wafers, AVGO's TSMC CoWoS wafers roughly 2.4x in 2026 and rise another ~60% in 2027.** In absolute wafers AVGO is the largest gainer after NVDA; the share line flattening near 22% is a denominator effect. **The share half is the negative: the reallocation goes to [[MEDIATEK]] (to 7.3% / 137,700 wafers, from Fubon's own 5.3%) and [[AMD]] (total allocation to 300k, majority via ASE), while [[NVDA]] holds flat at 50.0%.** Fubon states the MediaTek logic explicitly — *"we see that MediaTek is able to get more capacity allocation in 2027, and therefore, we think it is possible that MediaTek could further raise its custom-ASIC market share in 2027"* — and names **MediaTek its top pick for TPU**, calling the recent pullback a re-entry opportunity. That is the sharpest head-to-head statement on the page from a house that covers both. ⚠️ **Two guardrails. (i) This is TSMC-only CoWoS and a broker capacity assumption, not a TSMC or AVGO disclosure. (ii) It is a UNIT/wafer claim and says nothing about revenue share — MS (07-14, 07-26) still models AVGO at ~80% of TPU revenue on higher ASP and gross margin even while conceding MediaTek closes the unit gap. Do not read a wafer-share trim as a revenue-share trim.** **The offsetting positive for AVGO in the same note is on the networking side: ASE's FoCoS capacity is raised to 50kwpm for 2027 "to support CPU and networking customers' demand"** — up from Fubon's own 45k (06-05) and 30k before that, i.e. the offload platform that carries AVGO's switch chipsets and transceivers keeps expanding. Also relevant to the BOM debate this page tracks: Fubon puts **HBM4 at US$31-32/GB for NVDA but US$35-36/GB "for other GPU and custom-ASIC projects," i.e. roughly a doubling of HBM cost in 2027 — and AVGO sits on the wrong side of that spread**, which sharpens the standing HBM-pass-through GM headwind (see Risks / Debate). Fubon's overall stance is **more confident in the CPU, SPE and TPU chains than in the [[NVDA]] GPU chain** on Rubin's 2H26 volume cut. (Fubon "Our advanced packaging, CoWoS forecast adjustments; other updates", 2026-07-28) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/Our_advanced_packaging_CoWoS_forecast_adjustments_other_up.html) |
| 07-28 | Morgan Stanley TMT webcast · Joe Moore ("2Q26 memory, Intel implications and ARM preview") | valuation | bull | **Joe Moore puts AVGO second across his entire coverage: "My rank order would be Nvidia, Broadcom, then Micron, then Sandisk… those are the top four. And there's nothing else that's wildly compelling."** The argument is relative value, not a fresh estimate — the four "feel maybe unimaginative because they're the names we all associate with all of this, but they're **just so much cheaper than every other name that has some speculative potential**," and he intends to "continue to look to buy on these selloffs rather than be the most enthusiastic guys at the peak." One line only; no rating, PT or estimate change, and it is consistent with the MS OW $502 / ~80%-TPU-share defence already on this page (Moore is the same analyst who wrote "In Defense of AVGO's ASIC Share," 07-14). (Morgan Stanley webcast "2Q26 memory, Intel implications and ARM preview" · Joe Moore, 2026-07-28) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/2026_07_28_2q26_ms_memory_intel_implications_and_arm_preview.html) |
