| 07-24 | SemiAnalysis · Dylan Patel et al. ("Can AMD break the CUDA Moat? AMD Advancing AI 2026") | produto | **bull** | **The most concrete quantification yet of AVGO's scale-up switching socket — and it is sole-source at AMD.** AMD's 72-GPU **Helios** rack runs on **12 Broadcom Tomahawk 6 102.4T switch ASICs across 6 switch trays** (2/tray), single-tier all-to-all, 72 lanes of 200G UALoE per GPU (1.8 TB/s uni-di). AMD has no scale-up switch of its own: "**Because Broadcom is the only merchant provider shipping a 100T switch with 200G SerDes, Broadcom is AMD's only choice for the switch**" — SemiAnalysis frames this as AMD's constraint ("AMD cannot reach extreme codesign"), i.e. a locked socket for AVGO. The mismatch is extra AVGO content too: only **432 of each switch's 512 200G lanes are used** (86.4T of 102.4T) because merchant silicon doesn't divide evenly across 72 GPUs, vs Nvidia's purpose-built **28.8T NVSwitch** which does "with no wastage of bandwidth at all." **Net-new content leg:** AMD's weak 200G SerDes means "**up to 85% of its backplane needs to be retimed, requiring over 550 Broadcom ethernet retimers per rack**" (~85% of scale-up links on Meta's MI455X), sited in the scale-up switch tray — cost/power/assembly pain for AMD, incremental silicon for AVGO (Nvidia's Oberon backplane is fully passive). **MI500 (2027) scales the switch count ~24x:** co-packaged copper + near-packaged optics (9 optical engines × 6.4 Tbit/s = 57.6T scale-up), a flat one-tier **576-GPU / 8-rack** domain built from **288 switches on a 115.2T Tomahawk 6 SKU**. Also relevant to the frontier-lab-concentration thread: AMD is described as "**fighting a war on two fronts**" — merchant GPUs *and* the custom-ASIC complex (TPU/Trainium making external chip sales to large AI labs), i.e. the ASIC franchise AVGO builds is the second front. [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/2026_07_24_ENC_Can_AMD_break_the_CUDA_Moat_AMD_Advancing_AI_2026_email.html) |
| 07-21 | SemiAnalysis (Meta Infra piece — networking/PCIe read) | produto | mixed | **Complement to the 07-21 Rivos/COT row below — the same note is net-positive on AVGO's switching/PCIe sockets at Meta.** (1) **Grand Teton (H100 HGX):** Meta's custom switch tray housed **four Broadcom PCIe switches + 16 SSDs + eight NICs**, and SemiAnalysis's verdict is that Meta "paid more, had worse TCO, didn't reduce their reliance on Nvidia networking gear, and **increased their reliance on Broadcom**" (tray since cancelled — the checkpoint storage was never used as anticipated; note Nvidia designed standalone PCIe switches out of the reference HGX at Hopper via ConnectX-7, so this content was Meta-specific). (2) **DSF → NSF:** Meta's Disaggregated Scheduled Fabric ran on Broadcom **Jericho3-AI + Ramon3** deep-buffer silicon; as NICs absorbed RoCE congestion control the industry moved to Non-Scheduled Fabric on shallow-buffer **Tomahawk 5**, and **Prometheus and Hyperion now deploy "a vast majority of NSF instances"** — a **mix shift within AVGO's own portfolio**, not a lost fabric, though it opens the box to whiteboxes (Celestica) alongside Arista and SemiAnalysis models **DSF ~11% more expensive than NSF**. DSF persists where **MTIA** is used (NIC on-die); GB300 runs Nvidia NICs → NSF, so both co-exist at Meta. [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/2026_07_21_ENC_Metas_Infrastructure_Team_Needs_A_Culture_Reset_email.html) |
| 07-25 | **Reuters (announced 2026-07-25)** | supply | **bull** | 🔴 **Broadcom struck a $200bn / 5-year chip supply partnership with [[SAMSUNG]] covering memory, foundry AND advanced packaging** (Reuters: "Samsung Elec wins $200 billion Broadcom AI chip partnership, boosting foundry push"). Read from the AVGO side, this is a **supply-security and second-sourcing move, and it lands on the page's two live constraints at once**: (a) the CoWoS/advanced-packaging bottleneck at TSMC — this page already carries TSMC's own 2Q26 statement that **back-end sits in deeper shortage than front-end, with packaging capacity now limiting customer growth**; and (b) HBM/memory allocation, where NVDA's ability to outbid on memory has been the recurring worry in the ASIC-vs-GPU debate. Securing five years of Samsung memory + foundry + packaging capacity is exactly how a fabless custom-ASIC house de-risks a multi-year XPU ramp it has already sold to customers. **What it is NOT, until AVGO or Samsung says so: it is a reported partnership/supply-contract value, not AVGO backlog, and no node, product or program split has been disclosed** — BofA's own weekend desk note hedged it as "AVGO **possibly** doing $200B deal with Samsung foundry." Two cross-reads to hold rather than resolve: (1) **Hock Tan told JPM on 07-07 that Broadcom retains majority TPU volume share with Google and dismissed the MediaTek-share narrative as "Taiwan PR"** — a Samsung tie-up is consistent with that (it is capacity, not a customer shift), but it does raise the question of whether any Google TPU volume migrates off TSMC; (2) it is a **direct competitive datapoint against TSMC's turnkey position**, and the first time a top-tier fabless AI customer has bundled logic *and* packaging with Samsung at this scale. Samsung reports 7/29; AVGO's next print is the natural place for the split. |
