| 07-29 | Aurelion Research/PhotonCap · Lumentum mgmt meeting (read-through) | competicao | mixed | **Read-through only — subject company is Lumentum. Joint independent publication: management meeting + investment analysis by Aurelion Research, technical comments by PhotonCap; interview answers are the authors' paraphrase.** **Three AVGO marks. (1) On copper reach, Lumentum's VP-IR answers Broadcom directly: "Credo and Broadcom argue six meters is achievable. On paper it is, but the power you would have to push through that copper makes it impractical, so the switch to optical happens around that point regardless"** (Lumentum mgmt via Aurelion Research, 2026-07-29). PhotonCap's read: "Kathy's answer to the six meter claims concedes it: doable, but the power bill kills it." **(2) Aurelion notes "Broadcom has said that optical scale-up is not quite there yet"** — cited alongside Jensen's copper-for-scale-up / photonics-for-scale-out framing as evidence the copper-runs-out mechanism is "slightly misguided." **(3) The integration threat, which is the real AVGO angle here — Aurelion's own view, not management's: on whether the long-term winners in optics end up being "the silicon giants who can acquire an optics platform and integrate it on-chip," they judge Lumentum's telecom-survivorship defence "the weakest argument… Broadcom and Marvell have plenty of money, and they don't need the best laser in the world. They only need one that is good enough to put on their own chip."** **PhotonCap frames AVGO's dual role precisely: "Broadcom is a potential customer and at the same time a platform competitor with its own laser sources," while "Marvell can be a buyer, an ecosystem partner, and an integration layer competitor all at once. Absent a disclosed design win, though, these are demand paths, not confirmed customers."** ⚠️ **No design win, no revenue number, no dated program — this is a competitive-posture datapoint, not a modelling input.** Ties to the open question already on [COHR.md](COHR.md) (Citi 07-16) of whether Broadcom buys CPO components from Coherent or self-integrates. See [LITE.md](LITE.md), [themes/optical-cpo.md](themes/optical-cpo.md). |
