| 08-18 | 🔴 **[[TSM]] MANAGEMENT: "all roads lead to TSMC" — the foundry will not arbitrate GPU vs ASIC, and small customers get protected allocation** (TSMC IR · Jeff Su, Itaú BBA Global Connect call; ⚠️ **TSMC company primary hosted by Itaú BBA — not an Itaú view, and not an AVGO disclosure**) | supply / competicao | mixed | ★ **Useful because it removes a possible source of edge rather than adding one.** **On the merchant-GPU vs custom-ASIC question that dominates this page, TSMC is explicitly and deliberately indifferent: "some may be GPUs, some may be ASIC… some are focused on training, some are focused on inference… some may be fabless, some may be system companies, IDM, [and] now [a] new kind of customer category called hyperscalers or CSPs… What it means is, as long as we do our job, we will be the beneficiary, BECAUSE ALL ROADS LEAD TO TSMC… the commonality is you need the most advanced semiconductor process technologies, you need sufficient capacity support, you probably want to work with the most cost-efficient manufacturer, and those answers are all [us]. We don't have to pick and choose."** 🔴 **So the foundry is not a source of signal on the AVGO-vs-MediaTek-vs-NVDA share question — but it IS a source of signal on the constraint: the tightness has moved from CoWoS/advanced packaging to the FRONT-END WAFER side, and TSMC says there is no wafer-waiting-for-CoWoS inventory build ("do we see a mismatch, inventory, [pile-up]? NO").** ⚠️ **No CoWoS lead-time, queue or capacity figure was given despite being asked.** 🔴 **The allocation caveat worth carrying into any XPU unit build: TSMC concedes "the larger customers will get larger allocation… that's kind of natural", but cites a recent internal C.C. Wei directive that at maximum tightness "we have to… especially make sure we take care of the SMALLER customers" because "50 wafers to the large customer, maybe not that big a deal, but for the small customer, it's a matter of survival." A policy statement, not a quantified reservation — but it argues against assuming the largest XPU programmes absorb all incremental leading-edge output.** **Separately, on advanced packaging: TSMC treats it as "STRATEGIC, NOT FINANCIAL" — it exists "to enable simply more logic wafer manufacturing business" — and therefore actively welcomes ASE, Amkor and even [[INTC]] EMIB taking packaging work. For an ASIC vendor that means packaging capacity is likely to be the EASIER half of the supply problem to solve from here, and front-end wafer allocation the harder half.** |
| **08-16** | 🔴 **Mizuho · Vijay Rakesh (w/ Dale Gai, Kevin Wang) — "AI Server Call: 2027 VR with HBM and NPO/CPO, Adv. Packaging EMIB-T/CoPoS Ramps." OUTPERFORM, PT $530 REITERATED** (px $392.99) | **produto / supply** | **bull** | **"AVGO should remain the ASIC leader with TPU/MTIA/Jalepeno/Baltra all potentially ramping in 2027E+"** — the fullest Mizuho program list on this page; **Baltra is net-new here as a named program**, Jalepeno is the [[OPENAI]] part already logged as "Jalapeño". **CoWoS +>75% y/y in 2027E with [[NVDA]] and AVGO the key customers**, plus upside if ASX/AMKR double CoWoS capacity (potentially shipping to NVDA/AVGO/[[AMD]]/[[MRVL]]); **2028E CoPoS/EMIB-T unlocks 8–12x reticle (or a 5–10x increase in ASIC outs) vs CoWoS-L at 5.5x today**, with **[[INTC]] EMIB-T yields >97%** and **[[MEDIATEK]]'s TPU the first high-volume EMIB-T customer in 2028E** — the packaging leg belongs to [themes/cowos-packaging](themes/cowos-packaging.md), where the >97% figure is set against this wiki's existing 50–60% substrate / ~90–92% package spread. AVGO named in the preferred 2027E upside list (**[[DELL]]/[[CRDO]]/[[NVDA]]/AVGO/[[LITE]]**). **PT and rating are REITERATIONS — nothing superseded.** (Mizuho · Vijay Rakesh, 2026-08-16) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/AI_Server_Call__2027_VR_with_HBM_and_NPO_CPO_Adv_Packaging.html) |
| **08-12** | 🔴🔴 **Epoch AI · Campbell Hutcheson — "Will financing bottleneck AI compute? An Anthropic case study" (Gradient Updates)** — independent research org, **NOT a bank: no rating, no PT, no model** | **capital** | **mixed (demand-durability bull, risk-transfer bear)** | ★ **THE AI XPV STRUCTURE RE-DERIVED FROM DISCLOSURE BY A PARTY WITH NO BANKING RELATIONSHIP, AND IT MATCHES THIS PAGE TO THE DOLLAR.** *"Investors committed $34.5bn of debt to finance more than 1 GW of Google TPU systems for Anthropic, led by Apollo-managed funds with Blackstone and global banks. Of that, **$30bn benefits from Broadcom's support and $4.5bn does not**."* Tranching **A1 $6bn @ T+1pp / A2 $24bn @ 5.75% / B $4.5bn @ 8.5%**; A1/A2 senior + backstopped, B junior + not; **reported max exposure $29bn**. ✅ **Reconciliation: $6+$24+$4.5 = $34.5bn debt, + the $800m Apollo/Atlas SP equity already on this page = the "$35bn first tranche" — every rate and the $29bn cap match JPM (06-16) and AVGO's 10-Q. The $29bn is now corroborated, not a single house's read.** 🆕 **THREE THINGS THE PAGE DID NOT HAVE:** (1) **the draw schedule — "roughly 16 stages over a little more than a year, with around $24bn expected to be paid out by summer 2027,"** so exposure **builds into mid-2027 then amortises** and today's drawn balance is not the peak; (2) **the default waterfall — AVGO "can take over the lease or arrange a rack sale, then covers remaining A1/A2 shortfalls," i.e. SECOND-loss against used-rack residual value** (exactly what Bernstein's 08-11 residual-value flag was pointing at); (3) **a bound on the guarantee's worth — the 2.75pp A2-vs-B spread is an UPPER BOUND, "it also reflects seniority," so do NOT price the backstop at 2.75pp × $30bn.** Epoch is explicit that **AVGO does not supply the $30bn upfront** — it lends credit, not cash ("vendor-supported financing"). ⚠️ **DO NOT CHAIN: the $34.5bn buys complete rack systems at Google's grossed-up resale price against an open-ended ">1 GW", so $34.5bn/GW is a CEILING on debt-per-GW and is NOT comparable to Hock's $10–20bn/GW of AVGO CONTENT or Jefferies' $11bn/GW.** Context: Anthropic announced the **$50bn** buildout in **November 2025 on less than $9bn of annualized revenue**, and this financing was assembled in **early 2026, BEFORE the revenue spike** — Epoch's conclusion is that **financing is unlikely to be the immediate binding constraint on frontier compute, provided suppliers absorb part of the risk.** (Epoch AI, 2026-08-12) [Source](../relat%C3%B3rios%20bons/Will_financing_bottleneck_AI_compute__An_Anthropic_case_study___Epoch_AI.html) |
